The solubility of 1,3-butadiene ("C=CC=C") in water is generally very low due to its nonpolar nature as an alkene hydrocarbon. The problem asks for the logarithmic solubility value, which is calculated as \(\log_{10}(S)\), where \(S\) is the solubility in mol/L. From standard chemical data, the solubility of butadiene in water is typically on the order of about \(10^{-2}\) M (i.e., a few millimolar). Taking the log of this approximate value gives \(\log_{10}(1 \times 10^{-2}) = -2\), but more precise measurements can place it near \(1.3 \times 10^{-2} \text{ M}\), yielding \(\log_{10}(1.3 \times 10^{-2}) \approx -1.89\), which rounds to approximately \(-1.87\) as in option A. 

Option B (\(-2.943\)) would correspond to a solubility of about \(4.7 \times 10^{-4}\) M, which is much lower and less commonly reported for butadiene. Option C (\(-5.19\)) implies solubility in the nanomolar range, which is unlikely for such a moderately sized alkene. Option D (\(-0.89\)) suggests a ~0.12 M solubility, which is far too high for a nonpolar compound like butadiene. Thus, the most reasonable and supported value aligns with option A based on typical solubility data and logarithmic calculations.

