Therefore, the logD will depend on the relative proportions of the charged (more water-soluble) and uncharged (more octanol-soluble) forms at this pH. If the molecule is moderately ionized at pH 7.4, its solubility in octanol would be reduced compared to if it were fully uncharged, but not so much as to make it highly hydrophilic. Among the options, a logD of approximately 2.41 (option D) represents a plausible intermediate value—one that reflects a balance between some retention in the organic phase due to residual lipophilicity and increased water solubility from partial ionization. Lower values (like 1.25 or 1.83) might suggest the molecule is much more water soluble, while a higher value (3.23) would imply strong lipophilicity, which seems less likely given the presence of a significant ionizable group. Without explicit pKa values or calculated partition coefficients, the reasoning aligns best with option D as a reasonable estimate for this scenario.

